I read this book in Grade 10th with just a casual attitude, thinking it would be nothing serious. I felt that such books wouldn't really hook me, as I had not been much attracted to the web novels on Startings before.
Then... I fell into a trap.
According to this plot, the entire book is quite light and pleasant. Even when Ye Xin was still in the online game, the rivalries between guilds were not heavy because they were often derailed by Ye Shen's dramatic twists and turns.
Regarding the competition part, it captured a little bit of the bittersweet feeling of change and transition in times (which might be due to my sensitive nature).
Reading the whole book took me many high school study hours, but I couldn't stop.
The portrayal of full-time characters and emotional arcs is stunningly impressive, with some characters mentioned only briefly but leaving a deep impression. Characters like叶修, 黄少天, and 苏沐橙 have garnered many fans, while other non-central characters such as 江波涛 and 楚云秀 are also very likable. In a gaming爽文 (爽文 typically refers to a light-hearted, action-oriented web novel), it's remarkable that one cannot find an utterly detestable character. This is a success in the way Full Time Enemy portrays its characters.
While on the emotional front, full-time authors do not suffer from the common issue of harem girls in web novels (either beautiful or degenerate) that web novels often face, with everyone's emotions in the entire story sharing a commonality: they all like the game Honor and aspire to win honorably.
Small measures indicate no great conspiracy; everyone seems so simple and adorable.
This might also be why there are no truly unpleasant main characters.
Full-time IPs are large, in part due to this.
Leaving aside this cultural phenomenon of web literature, being a full-time writer could be a decent book.
How much he really does provide some passion and a little bit of touch that moves people.
Full-length anime production is scheduled for this April.
